
Indian Standards (BIS) Recommendation Engine - Project Analysis
This report provides a comprehensive, in-depth architectural and technical analysis of the Indian Standards (BIS) Recommendation Engine located in the workspace.

1. Project Overview & Objectives
The project is a 100% Offline, Zero-Hallucination AI Recommendation & Regulatory Compliance Engine tailored for the Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS). It is primarily designed to:

Take natural language queries, Hinglish queries, or uploaded tender documents (PDF/CSV) as input.
Accurately recommend the exact Indian Standard (IS Code) for materials, testing methods, or codes of practice.
Enforce statutory Quality Control Orders (QCO) to check if a standard is legally mandatory (e.g., requires ISI Mark, Hallmarking, or CRS).
Generate ready-to-use procurement clauses for the Government e-Marketplace (GeM).
Identify and map allied/connected standards (e.g., linking a concrete specification to its compressive strength testing method).
2. High-Level Architecture
The system operates as a decoupled client-server architecture:

Backend: A Python-based FastAPI application handling AI models, retrieval, ingestion, database management, and compliance checks.
Frontend: A modern React application built with Vite providing user interfaces for search, benchmark testing, and tender auditing.
Databases: Local SQLite for relational metadata (standards_master.db), FAISS for dense vector indices, and Pickle for BM25 sparse indices.
The architecture explicitly optimizes for offline capability (running locally without external cloud LLM dependencies) and hardware adaptability (dynamically scaling the reranking pool based on available CPU/GPU RAM).

3. Backend Deep Dive
The backend is modularized under backend/src/ with clear responsibilities:

A. Semantic & Lexical Retrieval (Hybrid Search)
Located in backend/src/retrieval/:

Lexical Search (BM25): bm25_lexical_indexer.py handles exact keyword matching for numerical specifications and acronyms.
Dense Vector Search (FAISS + BGE-M3): bge_multilingual_embedder.py and faiss_vector_indexer.py. It uses the BAAI/bge-m3 model which outputs 1024-dimensional vectors. Crucially, BGE-M3 supports over 100 languages, giving the engine native support for Hindi, Tamil, and colloquial Hinglish.
Hybrid Fusion: hybrid_search_orchestrator.py combines sparse (BM25) and dense (FAISS) candidate lists using Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) to balance exact text matches with semantic meaning.
Cross-Encoder Reranking: cross_encoder_reranker.py utilizes BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3 for deep pairwise cross-attention scoring. It incorporates a Dynamic Compute Ladder (Auto-Clamp) that scales down the number of evaluated pairs if running on a weaker CPU to keep latency under 2 seconds.
B. Anti-Hallucination & Query Processing
Adaptive Query Preprocessor: (query_preprocessor.py) Normalizes queries, strips out procurement boilerplate legalese (e.g., "Notice Inviting Tender for"), extracts material grades (e.g., 43 Grade), and classifies intent (Specification vs. Test Method).
IS-Code Whitelist Guard: A hard gate using is_code_whitelist.json ensuring the AI cannot hallucinate non-existent standard codes (e.g., IS 99999). If a candidate isn't in the official master registry, it is discarded.
Corrective RAG (CRAG): Deconstructs large standard scopes into atomic knowledge strips to provide a highly cited grounding rationale.
C. Compliance & Government Schemas
Located in backend/src/compliance/ and procurement/:

QCO Mandatory Engine: qco_mandatory_engine.py checks against a catalog (Scheme-I ISI, Scheme-II CRS, Scheme-IV Hallmarking) to flag if a standard is mandatory by law.
GeM Specification Generator: gem_specification_generator.py drafts copy-paste ready Government e-Marketplace procurement specifications.
Tender Auditor: tender_compliance_auditor.py and tender_document_parser.py use PyMuPDF to parse multi-page PDFs and CSV Bills of Quantities (BoQ) line-by-line, recommending standards for each item.
D. Relational Graph Database
Located in backend/src/database/:

SQLite Manager: sqlite_manager.py manages a self-contained local database (standards_master.db). It stores the standard registry, the QCO rules, and an edge-graph of allied standards (classifying relationships into Test Methods, Installation Codes, Safety, etc.).
4. Frontend Deep Dive
The frontend (frontend/src/) is a Single Page Application (SPA) that acts as the user interface for procurement officers and engineers.

Key React Components:

SpecSearch.jsx: The core search interface. It handles user input (including Hinglish) and visualizes the RRF/Cross-Encoder scores and confidence bands.
TenderAuditor.jsx: A drag-and-drop interface for uploading tender PDFs or CSVs. It displays a parsed table of Bill of Quantities (BoQ) items alongside their audited BIS compliance status and recommended standards.
StandardCard.jsx & StandardDetailsModal.jsx: Displays individual standard metadata, QCO warning banners (e.g., mandatory ISI mark), and interactive network graphs of allied standards.
GeMClauseModal.jsx: Provides the one-click generated legal procurement text for the government portal.
BenchmarkSandbox.jsx: A specialized UI for running evaluation suites (like national_evaluation_test_set.json) and displaying Hit@1 / MRR metrics visually.
5. Testing and Evaluation
The backend/datasets/ directory contains rigorous benchmark suites:

national_evaluation_test_set.json & public_test_set.json
The project demonstrates perfect (100%) accuracy on complex stress-tests, handling multi-part series disambiguation (e.g., IS 1239 Part 1 vs Part 2) and intent disambiguation.
End-to-End tests are implemented in scripts like test_tender_compliance_e2e.py and test_multilingual_and_natural_queries.py.
6. Project Structure Overview
text

IS-Recommendation-Engine/
├── backend/
│   ├── data/                 # SQLite DB, FAISS/BM25 indices, whitelists, scraped catalogs
│   ├── datasets/             # Evaluation benchmarks and test sets
│   ├── src/                  # Core Python Source Code
│   │   ├── api/              # FastAPI application (fastapi_application.py)
│   │   ├── compliance/       # QCO Engine, Tender Auditor
│   │   ├── database/         # SQLite schema and manager
│   │   ├── graph/            # Allied Standards relationship classifier
│   │   ├── ingestion/        # PDF/CSV Tender Parsers, Web Scrapers (Firecrawl)
│   │   ├── llm/              # Grounded Rationale generation
│   │   ├── localization/     # Multilingual support
│   │   ├── procurement/      # GeM Clause Generator
│   │   └── retrieval/        # Hybrid Search (BM25, FAISS, Cross-Encoder, Preprocessor)
│   └── scripts/              # Setup, data enrichment, parsing utilities
├── frontend/
│   ├── src/                  # React Application
│   │   ├── api/              # Axios client communicating with FastAPI
│   │   ├── components/       # UI Components (SpecSearch, TenderAuditor, etc.)
│   │   └── ...
├── ARCHITECTURE_AND_MODELS_GUIDE.md # Plain English breakdown of AI models
├── README.md                 # System overview and quick start guide
└── start.py / start.bat      # Unified launchers to boot both backend & frontend
7. Summary & Strengths
This project is exceptionally well-engineered for its domain (Government Procurement and Indian Standards). Its primary strengths lie in:

Domain-Specific Constraints: Instead of relying blindly on generative AI, it enforces a hard retrieval pipeline with strict lexical/semantic gating (Zero-Hallucination Gate).
Offline-First: All models (BGE-M3, BGE-Reranker) and databases (SQLite, FAISS) run locally, eliminating data privacy concerns for sensitive government tenders.
Advanced Retrieval Pipeline: The combination of Adaptive Query Preprocessing -> BM25 + FAISS -> RRF -> Cross-Encoder -> Material/Intent Precision alignment is state-of-the-art for semantic search architectures.
